{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-110547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution of ${2}^{4x}={3}^{1-x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-110547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4x} & ={3}^{1-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{1-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)=(1-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =(1-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-110547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c33bf02a2623fe2403b03e4ee65eaf85ece57ffa08ff62e354c3b018fcbb626"} {"question" "q-110547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-110547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-110547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-233244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${5}^{x-2}={7}^{3-2x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-233244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x-2} & ={7}^{3-2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x-2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)=(3-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(3-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-233244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c33bf02a2623fe2403b03e4ee65eaf85ece57ffa08ff62e354c3b018fcbb626"} {"question" "q-233244" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-233244" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\ln(5)+3\\ln(7)}{\\ln(5)+2\\ln(7)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{x+2}={2}^{x+3}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2} & ={2}^{x+3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+2=(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x+2 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c33bf02a2623fe2403b03e4ee65eaf85ece57ffa08ff62e354c3b018fcbb626"} {"question" "q-242902" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242902" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\ln(2)-2}{1-\\ln(2)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{2x-2}=2\\cdot {9}^{x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x-2} & =2\\cdot {9}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x-2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {9}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n2x-2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {9}^{x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{x}) \\\\ 2x-2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ 2x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) & =2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c33bf02a2623fe2403b03e4ee65eaf85ece57ffa08ff62e354c3b018fcbb626"} {"question" "q-242904" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242904" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2+\\ln(2)}{2-\\ln(9)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution of ${2}^{5x-1}={7}^{3-x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{5x-1} & ={7}^{3-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c33bf02a2623fe2403b03e4ee65eaf85ece57ffa08ff62e354c3b018fcbb626"} {"question" "q-242922" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037473/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef0ec2794b5661cf702bfeac476126943b2ab1539e275644f92dc53c0d4b7e2e"} {"question" "q-242922" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/math